.TH time "" "Command"
.SH NAME
time \- time the execution of a command
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtime [\fIcommand\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
time -- time the execution of a command
Usage:	time [ command ]
If no 'command', print the date.
.HE
.B time
runs
.IR command ,
and when it finishes, reports on the standard error the time it took,
broken down into three lines:
.B Real
(elapsed clock time),
.B User
(CPU time charged to
.I command
itself), and
.B Sys
(CPU time charged to the system on
.IR command 's
behalf), each given in minutes, seconds, and tenths of a second.
.PP
If
.I command
is omitted,
.B time
instead execs
.B /bin/date
and does no timing.
.PP
While
.I command
runs,
.B time
ignores the signals
.BR SIGHUP ,
.BR SIGINT ,
.BR SIGQUIT ,
and
.BR SIGTERM .
If
.I command
is killed by a signal,
.B time
reports the signal name (or number, if out of range) before printing
the timing lines, noting it if a core image was produced.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B date
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B time
prints the message `Try again.' and exits if it cannot fork, and
prints
.I command
followed by `: not found' if
.I command
cannot be executed.
.SH NOTES
.B time
is a separate command, not a shell built-in; it can only time a single
command, not a pipeline.
